same pointer value (now dangling).\"},{\"name\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1628\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (signed JL comparison, NO lower bound check)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[index]\",\"used_as\":\"read length (msg_size[index]-1)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[index] (heap buffer)\",\"source\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[index]-1\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"scanf\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"printf 'Which cookie?[0-%d]', scanf %llu into index. Check: if (index >= cookie_num) _abort. NOTE: comparison is 'cmp rdx,rax; jl skip' using signed JL — only checks upper bound. No check for index < 0. Then read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). Strips trailing newline.\"},{\"name\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1751\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (signed JL comparison, NO lower bound check)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"used_as\":\"printf(%s) argument\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"scanf\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"printf 'Which cookie?[0-%d]', scanf %llu into index. Check: if (index >= cookie_num) _abort. NOTE: same as edit — only upper bound, no lower bound. Then printf('%s', msg[index]).\"},{\"name\":\"menu\",\"address\":\"0x17fd\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"puts\"],\"summary\":\"Prints 7 lines of menu text via puts.\"},{\"name\":\"_abort\",\"address\":\"0x11f5\",\"callers\":[\"init\",\"create_cookie\",\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"msg\",\"source\":\"rdi argument\"}],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"],\"summary\":\"printf('%s', msg); exit(1). Fatal error handler.\"}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"WRITE to msg[index] heap buffer with size msg_size[index]-1\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Index is read as unsigned 64-bit via scanf(%llu) but only checked against upper bound (index < cookie_num) using signed JL. No lower-bound check. A large unsigned value (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) passes the signed JL check (interpreted as -1 < cookie_num) and indexes msg[] out of bounds, reaching msg_size[] and beyond in .bss. The read length comes from msg_size[index] which would be an out-of-bounds int, and the write target is msg[index] which is an out-of-bounds pointer dereference.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg[index] as printf %s argument\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Same missing lower-bound check as edit_cookie. Large unsigned index passes signed JL, causing out-of-bounds read from msg[] array. The read pointer is then passed to printf(%s), potentially leaking memory content.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom\",\"operation\":\"FREE msg[random_idx] then swap-remove\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"After free(msg[idx]), the last slot msg[cookie_num-1] is copied into msg[idx] but msg[cookie_num-1] is not cleared. The old cookie_num-1 slot retains the freed pointer. If cookie_num is later incremented (via create) without overwriting, the dangling pointer persists. Also, if random_idx == cookie_num-1 (self-swap), the freed pointer remains in the slot. Whether this leads to UAF depends on subsequent operations and S2 control flow analysis.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_length (scanf %lld)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(length,1) + read(0,buf,length)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"Length is checked <= 0x100. calloc zeros memory. read uses the same length. No obvious overflow. The newline strip writes buf[read_ret] = 0 which is within bounds.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE, Full RELRO, Canary, NX enabled, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8 at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg is a char* array at 0x4060, 32 elements (256 bytes), in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size is an int32 array at 0x4160, 32 elements (128 bytes), in .bss, immediately after msg\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is int64 at 0x41e8, initialized to 5 by init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() calls alarm(60), setvbuf on stdin/stdout/stderr, fopen /dev/urandom, and strdup 5 fortune strings into msg[0..4]\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num<=31 and length<=0x100, then calloc(length,1), read(0,buf,length), stores in msg[cookie_num]/msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, idx=byte%cookie_num, printf msg[idx], free(msg[idx]), swap msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx], cookie_num--. Freed pointer slot is overwritten by swap but old last slot not cleared.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu), checks only 'index >= cookie_num' using signed JL (cmp rdx,rax; jl). No lower bound check. Then read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu), checks only 'index >= cookie_num' using signed JL. No lower bound check. Then printf('%s', msg[index]).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"scanf format for index in edit_cookie/read_cookie is %llu (unsigned long long), stored in 8-byte stack local. The comparison instruction is 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' where rdx=user_index and rax=cookie_num.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060) and msg_size array (0x4160) are contiguous in .bss. msg_size starts exactly 256 bytes after msg. urandom is at 0x41e0 (128 bytes after msg_size). cookie_num at 0x41e8.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58, Full RELRO means GOT is read-only after relocation\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Runtime probe confirmed menu交互: option 2 prompts 'How long is the message?', accepts length, prompts 'Input your message:', reads message, prints 'Done!'. Option 4 prompts 'Which cookie?[0-N]:' and prints cookie content.\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"Since scanf(%llu) reads an unsigned 64-bit value and the bound check uses signed JL (index < cookie_num), a value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (-1 signed) passes the check. msg[-1] would access 8 bytes before msg (0x4058), which is in .bss padding area. Larger negative indices could reach .data or .got region.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, when random_idx equals cookie_num-1 (the last element), the swap copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment), leaving the freed pointer in place. Probability = 1/cookie_num per eat call.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie read length is msg_size[index]-1 (sub eax,0x1 then movsxd). If msg_size[index] is 0, subtraction yields -1, movsxd sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as read count — but read() syscall treats size_t, so this would be a very large read.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether the signed JL comparison on a %llu-scanned value actually allows negative indexing at runtime — depends on compiler codegen semantics of comparing unsigned-scanned value with signed JL\",\"reason\":\"The scanf stores full 64-bit unsigned value; JL compares as signed. Need S2/S3 dynamic verification to confirm a large unsigned value passes the check and produces OOB access.\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's swap-remove leaves a exploitable dangling pointer (UAF) depends on whether create_cookie reuses the slot and whether the freed pointer is accessible via edit/read before overwrite\",\"reason\":\"Requires S2 control flow analysis of create-after-eat sequences and whether the dangling slot index is reachable.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"seccomp/sandbox status unknown — no seccomp filter detected in static analysis but not runtime-verified\",\"reason\":\"No ptrace/seccomp syscalls observed in binary; would need runtime check.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Exact heap layout and whether tcache/fastbin placement can be controlled for exploitation\",\"reason\":\"Heap allocation sizes are user-controlled (1-256 via create_cookie). Glibc 2.39 uses tcache. Specific heap feng shui requires S3 dynamic analysis.\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size OOB read (when index is negative/OOB) yields a useful value for controlling read length in edit_cookie\",\"reason\":\"Depends on what data lies at msg_size[out_of_bounds_index], which requires knowing runtime .bss content.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped. Entry=0x1110. 11 program headers, 29 sections. BuildID=84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2. GCC 10.2.0. Source: fortunecookie.c.\",\"sections\":\".text@0x1110(0x891,RX) .rodata@0x2000(0x266,R) .got@0x3f58(0xa8,RW,FullRELRO) .data@0x4000(0x10,RW) .bss@0x4020(0x1d0,RW) .plt@0x1020(0xe0,RX) .init@0x1000 .fini@0x19a4 .init_array@0x3d58 .fini_array@0x3d60 .dynamic@0x3d68 .eh_frame@0x22e0 .eh_frame_hdr@0x2268\",\"symbols\":\"main@0x187f init@0x1232 create_cookie@0x1399 eat_cookie@0x14e1 edit_cookie@0x1628 read_cookie@0x1751 menu@0x17fd _abort@0x11f5. Globals: msg@0x4060(256B) msg_size@0x4160(128B) urandom@0x41e0(8B) cookie_num@0x41e8(8B) stdout@0x4020 stdin@0x4030 stderr@0x4040\",\"disassembly\":\"main: loop menu+scanf(%lld)+dispatch(1=eat,2=create,3=edit,4=read,5=exit). init: setvbuf*3+alarm(60)+fopen(/dev/urandom)+strdup*5->msg[0..4]+cookie_num=5. create_cookie: check cookie_num<=31, scanf(%lld) len<=0x100, calloc(len,1), read(0,buf,len), strip \\\\n, msg[cookie_num]=ptr, msg_size[cookie_num]=len, cookie_num++. eat_cookie: fread 1B from urandom, idx=byte%cookie_num, printf(msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--. edit_cookie: printf Which cookie, scanf(%llu) idx, cmp idx,cookie_num; jl ok (NO lower bound), read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1), strip \\\\n. read_cookie: printf Which cookie, scanf(%llu) idx, cmp idx,cookie_num; jl ok (NO lower bound), printf(%s,msg[idx]).\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_INDEX_CHECK\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie and read_cookie, the cookie index is read via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as an unsigned 64-bit integer, but the bounds check 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' uses the signed JL (jump if less) instruction. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is interpreted as -1 in signed comparison, which is less than cookie_num (always positive), so the upper-bound check is bypassed. There is no lower-bound check at all. The attacker-controlled index is then used to access the msg[] pointer array and msg_size[] int array out of bounds, leading to OOB pointer dereference and OOB size read.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (char* array at 0x4060, 32 elements, 256 bytes in .bss)\",\"msg_size (int32 array at 0x4160, 32 elements, 128 bytes in .bss)\",\"cookie_num (int64 at 0x41e8 in .bss)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") — unsigned 64-bit integer from stdin\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker sends menu choice 3 (edit) or 4 (read)\",\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &index) stores full 64-bit unsigned value on stack at [rbp-0x10]\",\"index loaded into rdx: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]'\",\"cookie_num loaded into rax: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]'\",\"Comparison: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl skip_abort' — JL is signed less-than; if index is interpreted as negative signed, it passes\",\"In edit_cookie: index used to compute msg_size[index] via 'lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax]' — OOB read of size value\",\"In edit_cookie: index used to compute msg[index] via 'lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]' — OOB pointer dereference\",\"In edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) — OOB write to whatever address the OOB pointer contains\",\"In read_cookie: index used to compute msg[index] via same addressing — OOB pointer dereference\",\"In read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) — OOB read printed to stdout\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as 0 <= index < cookie_num, using unsigned comparison (JBE/JB) or explicit lower+upper bound check\",\"actual\":\"Only upper bound checked via 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed JL). No lower bound check. scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") stores unsigned 64-bit value, but JL treats it as signed.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). Incorrect comparison type: signed JL instead of unsigned JB/JBE on a value read as unsigned. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (-1 signed) passes 'jl' because -1 < cookie_num.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access via signedness mismatch\",\"address\":\"edit_cookie: 0x1681 (cmp/jl check), 0x16a7-0x16de (msg_size[index] and msg[index] dereference + read write); read_cookie: 0x17aa (cmp/jl check), 0x17bb-0x17e1 (msg[index] dereference + printf)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) where index is OOB; read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) where index is OOB\",\"target\":\"msg[] array at 0x4060 (32 x 8-byte pointers) and msg_size[] array at 0x4160 (32 x 4-byte ints) in .bss. Negative indices reach .bss data before msg (0x4058 and earlier), potentially reaching .data (0x4000) and .got (0x3f58) regions.\",\"size\":\"edit_cookie: write size = msg_size[index]-1 (OOB int value, potentially very large if OOB slot contains 0 — see BUG-003); read_cookie: read size unbounded (printf %s reads until null terminator)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, a negative/OOB index causes msg[index] to be read from out-of-bounds .bss memory. The value at that location is used as the destination pointer for read(0, ptr, msg_size[index]-1). If the OOB pointer value is a valid writable address, attacker-controlled data from stdin is written there. The write size comes from msg_size[index] which is also OOB.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, a negative/OOB index causes msg[index] to be read from out-of-bounds .bss memory. The value at that location is passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\", ptr), which dereferences it and prints memory content until a null byte. This leaks memory content to stdout.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) in read_cookie with an OOB index can print the content of an arbitrary pointer found in .bss, potentially leaking heap addresses, libc addresses, or other sensitive data stored in .bss/.data sections.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB pointer value at msg[index] is not a valid mapped address, dereferencing it (in read() or printf()) causes SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1671\",\"description\":\"scanf format string at rodata 0x21c4 is \\\"%llu\\\" (unsigned long long), confirmed by objdump -s .rodata showing bytes 25 6c 6c 75 at offset 0x21c4. The scanned value is stored as full 64-bit unsigned.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]' (user index), 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]' (cookie_num), 'cmp rdx, rax', 'jl 0x1692'. JL (0F 8C) is jump-if-less signed. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is -1 signed, which is < cookie_num (positive), so the check passes. No lower-bound check exists.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16de\",\"description\":\"OOB index used for array access: 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0]' + 'lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] # 4160 <msg_size>' computes &msg_size[index] (OOB if index negative). 'mov eax,[rdx+rax]' reads OOB int. Then 'sub eax,0x1; movsxd rdx,eax' computes read length. 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0]' + 'lea rax,[rip+0x298e] # 4060 <msg>' computes &msg[index] (OOB). 'mov rax,[rcx+rax]' dereferences OOB pointer. 'call read@plt' writes to that pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179a-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Same bounds check pattern as edit_cookie: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]', 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]', 'cmp rdx, rax', 'jl 0x17bb'. Same signed JL on unsigned-scanned value. No lower-bound check.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"OOB index used: 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]' + 'lea rax,[rip+0x2892] # 4060 <msg>' computes &msg[index] (OOB). 'mov rax,[rdx+rax]' dereferences OOB pointer. 'mov rsi,rax' passes it as printf arg. Format string at 0x21a9 is \\\"%s\\\".\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"description\":\"msg array at 0x4060, msg_size at 0x4160, both in .bss. Negative index -1 accesses msg[-1] at 0x4058 (8 bytes before msg, in .bss padding). Larger negative indices reach .data (0x4000) and .got (0x3f58). The .bss layout is: stdout(0x4020), stdin(0x4030), stderr(0x4040), completed.0(0x4048), [padding 0x4049-0x405f], msg(0x4060). Index -1 → 0x4058, index -3 → 0x4048 (completed.0), index -4 → 0x4040 (stderr FILE*), index -5 → 0x4038, index -6 → 0x4030 (stdin FILE*), index -8 → 0x4020 (stdout FILE*). These FILE* pointers are valid heap addresses, so read_cookie would print FILE struct content (info leak), and edit_cookie would write to the FILE struct (memory corruption).\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Dynamic verification that scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") with input like 18446744073709551615 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) actually passes the JL check at runtime\",\"Dynamic verification of what memory content is at msg[-1] through msg[-8] at runtime (FILE* pointers in .bss)\",\"Dynamic verification that the OOB pointer dereference in read_cookie produces readable output or crash\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"HEAP_USE_AFTER_FREE_VIA_SWAP_REMOVE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, after free(msg[idx]), the function performs swap-remove: msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] and msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1], then cookie_num--. However, msg[cookie_num-1] (the old last slot) is NOT cleared/nullified after the swap. When idx == cookie_num-1 (self-swap case), the freed pointer is copied onto itself, leaving the dangling pointer in the slot. Even when idx != cookie_num-1, the old last slot retains the freed pointer value. After cookie_num is decremented, the dangling slot is at index cookie_num (now out of the valid range), but a subsequent create_cookie increments cookie_num and writes to msg[cookie_num] — if the new cookie_num equals the old freed slot index, the slot is overwritten before use. However, if the self-swap case occurs (idx == cookie_num-1), the freed pointer remains at the SAME index that is still within the valid range [0, cookie_num-1], making it directly reachable via edit_cookie or read_cookie without needing create_cookie.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (char* array at 0x4060 in .bss)\",\"cookie_num (int64 at 0x41e8 in .bss)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom (1 byte, modulo cookie_num)\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie: fread 1 byte from /dev/urandom into stack var\",\"idx = byte % cookie_num (via idiv at 0x153e)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) — prints cookie content\",\"free(msg[idx]) at 0x158e — heap buffer freed\",\"msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15b0-0x15c6 — swap last element into freed slot\",\"msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15e0-0x15fc — swap size\",\"cookie_num-- at 0x1606-0x160a\",\"If idx == old cookie_num-1 (self-swap): msg[idx] still holds the freed pointer (self-assignment)\",\"The slot idx is still in valid range [0, new cookie_num-1] since idx < old cookie_num and new cookie_num = old cookie_num - 1, so idx <= new cookie_num - 1\",\"Attacker can now call edit_cookie(idx) or read_cookie(idx) to access the freed buffer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After free(msg[idx]), the freed slot should be cleared (msg[idx] = NULL) or the swap should also null out the old last slot (msg[cookie_num-1] = NULL).\",\"actual\":\"Swap copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] but does NOT null msg[cookie_num-1]. In self-swap case (idx == cookie_num-1), the freed pointer remains in the slot.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing nullification of the source slot after swap-copy. The freed pointer remains reachable when self-swap occurs.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Dangling pointer dereference after free\",\"address\":\"eat_cookie: free at 0x158e, swap at 0x15b0-0x15c6, no null at old slot\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; /* msg[cookie_num-1] NOT cleared */\",\"target\":\"Heap buffer previously allocated by calloc in create_cookie or strdup in init\",\"size\":\"Original allocation size (1-256 bytes for create_cookie, or strdup length for init cookies)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed heap buffer is accessed via read_cookie after eat_cookie self-swap, printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) reads from the freed buffer. If the heap allocator has reused the memory for another allocation, this reads data from the new allocation (info leak). If not yet reused, reads stale data.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed heap buffer is accessed via edit_cookie after eat_cookie self-swap, read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) writes attacker-controlled data into the freed buffer. If the heap allocator has reused the memory, this corrupts the new allocation.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Writing to a freed heap buffer that has been reallocated for a different purpose corrupts the new allocation's data, potentially affecting heap metadata or other heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed buffer's memory has been unmapped or if heap metadata is corrupted by the write, subsequent heap operations may crash.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"free(msg[idx]) called: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1]' loads msg[idx], 'mov rdi,rax', 'call free@plt'. The pointer is freed.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1593-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Swap-remove: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]; sub rax,0x1' computes cookie_num-1. 'lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]' loads msg[cookie_num-1]. Then 'lea rcx,[rdx*8]; lea rdx,[rip+msg]; mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx],rax' stores it into msg[idx]. There is NO instruction that sets msg[cookie_num-1] to NULL after the copy.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1530-0x1544\",\"description\":\"idx = fread_byte % cookie_num. When cookie_num is N, idx can be 0..N-1. The probability that idx == cookie_num-1 (self-swap) is 1/N per eat call. With initial cookie_num=5, probability is 1/5 = 20% per eat call. After multiple eat calls, the cumulative probability of at least one self-swap increases.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1606-0x160a\",\"description\":\"After swap, cookie_num is decremented: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]; sub rax,0x1; mov QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num],rax'. In the self-swap case (idx == old_cookie_num-1), after decrement the new cookie_num = old_cookie_num - 1 = idx. So idx == new cookie_num, which means idx is now OUT of valid range [0, new_cookie_num-1]. Wait — idx = old_cookie_num-1, new_cookie_num = old_cookie_num-1, so idx = new_cookie_num, which is NOT < new_cookie_num. So the self-swap slot is actually at index == new cookie_num, which is out of range. BUT: if the attacker then calls create_cookie, cookie_num increments back to old_cookie_num, and msg[old_cookie_num-1] (the dangling slot) is overwritten by the new allocation. So the UAF window is: after eat (self-swap), before next create at that slot — the dangling pointer is at index == cookie_num, which is NOT directly reachable via edit/read (which check index < cookie_num). The UAF is reachable only if create_cookie is called (incrementing cookie_num, making the slot valid again) but create_cookie OVERWRITES the slot with a new pointer. So the dangling pointer is overwritten before it can be accessed. Revised: the self-swap case does NOT leave a directly reachable dangling pointer because the slot index equals the new cookie_num (out of range). The non-self-swap case leaves the dangling pointer at old cookie_num-1, which is also now == new cookie_num (out of range). In both cases, the dangling slot is at index == cookie_num after decrement, which is out of the valid range. The dangling pointer becomes reachable only if create_cookie is called, but create_cookie writes to msg[cookie_num] (overwriting the dangling pointer) before incrementing. So the dangling pointer is always overwritten before becoming reachable. This means the UAF is NOT directly exploitable through normal edit/read paths.\"}],\"confidence\":\"LOW\",\"verification_required\":[\"Dynamic verification of whether the dangling pointer at msg[old_cookie_num-1] can be reached before create_cookie overwrites it — specifically whether any code path accesses msg[cookie_num] without first writing to it\",\"Analysis of whether the OOB signedness bug (BUG-001) can be combined with eat_cookie to reach the dangling slot at index == cookie_num (which would pass the signed JL check if interpreted as a large unsigned value, but cookie_num is small and positive, so index == cookie_num would NOT pass JL since cookie_num is not < cookie_num)\",\"Re-examination of whether there is any other code path that accesses msg[] at index == cookie_num without going through create_cookie first\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_READ_LENGTH\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, the read length is computed as msg_size[index] - 1. The instruction sequence is: 'mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax]' (load msg_size[index] as int32), 'sub eax, 0x1' (subtract 1), 'movsxd rdx, eax' (sign-extend int32 to int64). If msg_size[index] is 0, the subtraction yields -1 (0xFFFFFFFF as int32), and movsxd sign-extends it to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (int64). This value is passed as the size_t count argument to read(0, buf, count). The read() syscall interprets size_t as unsigned, so 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is a massive read count, allowing a buffer overflow far beyond the allocated buffer size.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size (int32 array at 0x4160 in .bss)\",\"msg (char* array at 0x4060 in .bss)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") — combined with BUG-001 OOB access, or with a valid index whose msg_size happens to be 0\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker calls edit_cookie with an index (possibly OOB via BUG-001)\",\"msg_size[index] is loaded as int32: 'mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax]' at 0x16b6\",\"Subtract 1: 'sub eax, 0x1' at 0x16b9 — if msg_size[index] == 0, eax becomes -1 (0xFFFFFFFF)\",\"Sign-extend to 64-bit: 'movsxd rdx, eax' at 0x16bc — rdx becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"This is passed as the count argument to read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de\",\"read() interprets count as size_t (unsigned), attempting to read up to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF bytes into msg[index] buffer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The read length should be validated to be > 0 before use, or msg_size[index] should be checked to be >= 1 before subtracting 1.\",\"actual\":\"No validation of msg_size[index] before subtraction. If msg_size[index] is 0 (which can happen via OOB access to uninitialized .bss memory, or if a cookie with length 0 is created), the subtraction underflows.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing check for msg_size[index] > 0 before computing msg_size[index] - 1. No clamping of the read length to the actual buffer size.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Integer underflow leading to oversized read\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], (size_t)(int64)(msg_size[index] - 1)) where msg_size[index] can be 0\",\"target\":\"Heap buffer pointed to by msg[index]\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (if msg_size[index] == 0), effectively unbounded\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If msg_size[index] is 0 (via OOB access to .bss memory containing zeros, or via a valid cookie with length 0), the read length underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. The read() call writes attacker-controlled data from stdin into the heap buffer at msg[index] with effectively no size limit, overflowing into adjacent heap memory.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The oversized write corrupts heap memory beyond the allocated buffer, potentially damaging heap metadata and adjacent heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Writing beyond the heap buffer will eventually hit unmapped memory, causing SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16bc\",\"description\":\"Read length computation: 'mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax]' loads msg_size[index] as 32-bit int. 'sub eax, 0x1' subtracts 1. If msg_size[index] == 0, eax = 0xFFFFFFFF (-1 as int32). 'movsxd rdx, eax' sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. This is passed as the 3rd argument (rdx) to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"read(0, msg[index], rdx) where rdx = sign-extended (msg_size[index] - 1). The read@plt call at 0x1090 receives this as size_t count. No bounds check on the computed length before the call.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4160\",\"description\":\"msg_size array at 0x4160 in .bss. For OOB indices (via BUG-001), msg_size[negative_index] accesses .bss memory before msg_size. The region 0x4000-0x415f includes .data (0x4000-0x400f) and .bss padding (0x4049-0x405f) and msg array content (0x4060-0x415f). Many of these bytes are zero (uninitialized .bss), so msg_size[OOB_index] is likely 0, triggering the underflow. Even for in-bounds indices, if create_cookie is called with length=0 (scanf %lld reads 0, which passes the <=0x100 check), calloc(0,1) returns a valid pointer, msg_size[slot]=0, and edit_cookie on that slot triggers the underflow.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Dynamic verification that create_cookie accepts length=0 (scanf %lld reads 0, cmp 0,0x100; jle passes, calloc(0,1) returns non-NULL)\",\"Dynamic verification that edit_cookie on a slot with msg_size=0 produces the underflow and oversized read\",\"Dynamic verification of what .bss memory values exist at OOB msg_size indices at runtime\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"Length check and buffer allocation\",\"reason\":\"create_cookie checks length <= 0x100 via 'cmp rax,0x100; jle' (signed JLE, but 0x100 is small positive, so signed/unsigned doesn't matter for values 0-256). calloc(length,1) zeros memory. read(0,buf,length) uses the same length. The newline strip writes buf[read_ret]=0 which is within bounds (read_ret <= length). No overflow in the allocation or read. Note: length=0 is valid (passes <=0x100), calloc(0,1) returns a minimum-size allocation, but the read(0,buf,0) reads 0 bytes — no overflow here. The issue with length=0 arises in edit_cookie (BUG-003), not in create_cookie itself.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu dispatch\",\"reason\":\"main reads choice via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and dispatches 1-5 using cmp/jne. Choices outside 1-5 fall through to the loop again (no action). No vulnerability in the dispatch logic.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Initialization\",\"reason\":\"init calls setvbuf, alarm(60), fopen, and strdup 5 fortune strings. All sizes are hardcoded constants. No user input. No vulnerability.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie swap-remove UAF (BUG-002) is directly exploitable\",\"reason\":\"Static analysis shows that after eat_cookie, the dangling pointer is at index == new cookie_num (out of valid range for edit/read). create_cookie overwrites this slot before it becomes reachable. The self-swap case also leaves the pointer at index == new cookie_num. No code path was found that accesses msg[cookie_num] without first writing to it via create_cookie. However, the OOB signedness bug (BUG-001) might allow reaching index == cookie_num if a large unsigned value is interpreted as a small positive number — but this is not possible since the value must be < cookie_num (signed JL), and cookie_num itself is a small positive number, so index must be strictly less than cookie_num.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic verification: after eat_cookie self-swap, attempt to access the dangling slot via edit_cookie or read_cookie with index == cookie_num to confirm it is rejected by the bounds check. Also verify whether any sequence of eat/create operations can leave a dangling pointer at an in-range index.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB access via BUG-001 can reach useful data in .got or .data sections\",\"reason\":\"msg is at 0x4060. .got is at 0x3f58 (0x108 bytes before msg, i.e., index -33 to -34 approximately). .data is at 0x4000 (0x60 bytes before msg, index -12 to -13). The exact values at these OOB indices at runtime (FILE* pointers, GOT entries) need dynamic verification to determine what pointer values are dereferenced and what sizes are read.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic verification: run the binary and dump .bss/.data/.got content at OOB msg[] indices to determine what pointer values and msg_size values exist at negative indices.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[],\"target_bug\":\"\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
  "vulnerability_report": {
    "schema": "VulnerabilityReport",
    "version": "1.0",
    "status": "COMPLETED",
    "summary": {
      "total_bugs": 3,
      "high_severity": 2,
      "medium_severity": 1,
      "low_severity": 0
    },
    "vulnerabilities": [
      {
        "bug_id": "BUG-001",
        "title": "OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_INDEX_CHECK",
        "root_cause": {
          "type": "Signedness Error",
          "description": "In edit_cookie and read_cookie, the cookie index is read via scanf(\"%llu\") as an unsigned 64-bit integer, but the bounds check 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' uses the signed JL (jump if less) instruction. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is interpreted as -1 in signed comparison, which is less than cookie_num (always positive), so the upper-bound check is bypassed. There is no lower-bound check at all. The attacker-controlled index is then used to access the msg[] pointer array and msg_size[] int array out of bounds, leading to OOB pointer dereference and OOB size read."
        },
        "affected_functions": [
          "edit_cookie",
          "read_cookie"
        ],
        "affected_objects": [
          "msg (char* array at 0x4060, 32 elements, 256 bytes in .bss)",
          "msg_size (int32 array at 0x4160, 32 elements, 128 bytes in .bss)",
          "cookie_num (int64 at 0x41e8 in .bss)"
        ],
        "attack_surface": {
          "input": "cookie_index via scanf(\"%llu\") — unsigned 64-bit integer from stdin",
          "entry_function": "edit_cookie / read_cookie",
          "data_flow": [
            "Attacker sends menu choice 3 (edit) or 4 (read)",
            "scanf(\"%llu\", &index) stores full 64-bit unsigned value on stack at [rbp-0x10]",
            "index loaded into rdx: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]'",
            "cookie_num loaded into rax: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]'",
            "Comparison: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl skip_abort' — JL is signed less-than; if index is interpreted as negative signed, it passes",
            "In edit_cookie: index used to compute msg_size[index] via 'lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax]' — OOB read of size value",
            "In edit_cookie: index used to compute msg[index] via 'lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]' — OOB pointer dereference",
            "In edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) — OOB write to whatever address the OOB pointer contains",
            "In read_cookie: index used to compute msg[index] via same addressing — OOB pointer dereference",
            "In read_cookie: printf(\"%s\", msg[index]) — OOB read printed to stdout"
          ]
        },
        "security_check": {
          "expected": "Index should be validated as 0 <= index < cookie_num, using unsigned comparison (JBE/JB) or explicit lower+upper bound check",
          "actual": "Only upper bound checked via 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed JL). No lower bound check. scanf(\"%llu\") stores unsigned 64-bit value, but JL treats it as signed.",
          "missing_or_incorrect": "Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). Incorrect comparison type: signed JL instead of unsigned JB/JBE on a value read as unsigned. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (-1 signed) passes 'jl' because -1 < cookie_num."
        },
        "vulnerable_operation": {
          "operation": "OOB array access via signedness mismatch",
          "address": "edit_cookie: 0x1681 (cmp/jl check), 0x16a7-0x16de (msg_size[index] and msg[index] dereference + read write); read_cookie: 0x17aa (cmp/jl check), 0x17bb-0x17e1 (msg[index] dereference + printf)",
          "expression": "edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) where index is OOB; read_cookie: printf(\"%s\", msg[index]) where index is OOB",
          "target": "msg[] array at 0x4060 (32 x 8-byte pointers) and msg_size[] array at 0x4160 (32 x 4-byte ints) in .bss. Negative indices reach .bss data before msg (0x4058 and earlier), potentially reaching .data (0x4000) and .got (0x3f58) regions.",
          "size": "edit_cookie: write size = msg_size[index]-1 (OOB int value, potentially very large if OOB slot contains 0 — see BUG-003); read_cookie: read size unbounded (printf %s reads until null terminator)"
        },
        "impact": [
          {
            "type": "OOB_WRITE",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "In edit_cookie, a negative/OOB index causes msg[index] to be read from out-of-bounds .bss memory. The value at that location is used as the destination pointer for read(0, ptr, msg_size[index]-1). If the OOB pointer value is a valid writable address, attacker-controlled data from stdin is written there. The write size comes from msg_size[index] which is also OOB."
          },
          {
            "type": "OOB_READ",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "In read_cookie, a negative/OOB index causes msg[index] to be read from out-of-bounds .bss memory. The value at that location is passed to printf(\"%s\", ptr), which dereferences it and prints memory content until a null byte. This leaks memory content to stdout."
          },
          {
            "type": "INFO_LEAK",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "The printf(\"%s\", msg[index]) in read_cookie with an OOB index can print the content of an arbitrary pointer found in .bss, potentially leaking heap addresses, libc addresses, or other sensitive data stored in .bss/.data sections."
          },
          {
            "type": "CRASH",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "If the OOB pointer value at msg[index] is not a valid mapped address, dereferencing it (in read() or printf()) causes SIGSEGV."
          }
        ],
        "evidence": [
          {
            "id": "E001",
            "type": "STATIC",
            "function": "edit_cookie",
            "address": "0x1671",
            "description": "scanf format string at rodata 0x21c4 is \"%llu\" (unsigned long long), confirmed by objdump -s .rodata showing bytes 25 6c 6c 75 at offset 0x21c4. The scanned value is stored as full 64-bit unsigned."
          },
          {
            "id": "E002",
            "type": "STATIC",
            "function": "edit_cookie",
            "address": "0x1681-0x1684",
            "description": "Bounds check: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]' (user index), 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]' (cookie_num), 'cmp rdx, rax', 'jl 0x1692'. JL (0F 8C) is jump-if-less signed. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is -1 signed, which is < cookie_num (positive), so the check passes. No lower-bound check exists."
          },
          {
            "id": "E003",
            "type": "STATIC",
