same pointer value (now dangling).\"},{\"name\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1628\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (signed JL comparison, NO lower bound check)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[index]\",\"used_as\":\"read length (msg_size[index]-1)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[index] (heap buffer)\",\"source\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[index]-1\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"scanf\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"printf 'Which cookie?[0-%d]', scanf %llu into index. Check: if (index >= cookie_num) _abort. NOTE: comparison is 'cmp rdx,rax; jl skip' using signed JL — only checks upper bound. No check for index < 0. Then read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). Strips trailing newline.\"},{\"name\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1751\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (signed JL comparison, NO lower bound check)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"used_as\":\"printf(%s) argument\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"scanf\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"printf 'Which cookie?[0-%d]', scanf %llu into index. Check: if (index >= cookie_num) _abort. NOTE: same as edit — only upper bound, no lower bound. Then printf('%s', msg[index]).\"},{\"name\":\"menu\",\"address\":\"0x17fd\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"puts\"],\"summary\":\"Prints 7 lines of menu text via puts.\"},{\"name\":\"_abort\",\"address\":\"0x11f5\",\"callers\":[\"init\",\"create_cookie\",\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"msg\",\"source\":\"rdi argument\"}],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"],\"summary\":\"printf('%s', msg); exit(1). Fatal error handler.\"}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"WRITE to msg[index] heap buffer with size msg_size[index]-1\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Index is read as unsigned 64-bit via scanf(%llu) but only checked against upper bound (index < cookie_num) using signed JL. No lower-bound check. A large unsigned value (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) passes the signed JL check (interpreted as -1 < cookie_num) and indexes msg[] out of bounds, reaching msg_size[] and beyond in .bss. The read length comes from msg_size[index] which would be an out-of-bounds int, and the write target is msg[index] which is an out-of-bounds pointer dereference.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg[index] as printf %s argument\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Same missing lower-bound check as edit_cookie. Large unsigned index passes signed JL, causing out-of-bounds read from msg[] array. The read pointer is then passed to printf(%s), potentially leaking memory content.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom\",\"operation\":\"FREE msg[random_idx] then swap-remove\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"After free(msg[idx]), the last slot msg[cookie_num-1] is copied into msg[idx] but msg[cookie_num-1] is not cleared. The old cookie_num-1 slot retains the freed pointer. If cookie_num is later incremented (via create) without overwriting, the dangling pointer persists. Also, if random_idx == cookie_num-1 (self-swap), the freed pointer remains in the slot. Whether this leads to UAF depends on subsequent operations and S2 control flow analysis.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_length (scanf %lld)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(length,1) + read(0,buf,length)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"Length is checked <= 0x100. calloc zeros memory. read uses the same length. No obvious overflow. The newline strip writes buf[read_ret] = 0 which is within bounds.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE, Full RELRO, Canary, NX enabled, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8 at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg is a char* array at 0x4060, 32 elements (256 bytes), in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size is an int32 array at 0x4160, 32 elements (128 bytes), in .bss, immediately after msg\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is int64 at 0x41e8, initialized to 5 by init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() calls alarm(60), setvbuf on stdin/stdout/stderr, fopen /dev/urandom, and strdup 5 fortune strings into msg[0..4]\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num<=31 and length<=0x100, then calloc(length,1), read(0,buf,length), stores in msg[cookie_num]/msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, idx=byte%cookie_num, printf msg[idx], free(msg[idx]), swap msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx], cookie_num--. Freed pointer slot is overwritten by swap but old last slot not cleared.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu), checks only 'index >= cookie_num' using signed JL (cmp rdx,rax; jl). No lower bound check. Then read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu), checks only 'index >= cookie_num' using signed JL. No lower bound check. Then printf('%s', msg[index]).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"scanf format for index in edit_cookie/read_cookie is %llu (unsigned long long), stored in 8-byte stack local. The comparison instruction is 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' where rdx=user_index and rax=cookie_num.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060) and msg_size array (0x4160) are contiguous in .bss. msg_size starts exactly 256 bytes after msg. urandom is at 0x41e0 (128 bytes after msg_size). cookie_num at 0x41e8.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58, Full RELRO means GOT is read-only after relocation\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Runtime probe confirmed menu交互: option 2 prompts 'How long is the message?', accepts length, prompts 'Input your message:', reads message, prints 'Done!'. Option 4 prompts 'Which cookie?[0-N]:' and prints cookie content.\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"Since scanf(%llu) reads an unsigned 64-bit value and the bound check uses signed JL (index < cookie_num), a value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (-1 signed) passes the check. msg[-1] would access 8 bytes before msg (0x4058), which is in .bss padding area. Larger negative indices could reach .data or .got region.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, when random_idx equals cookie_num-1 (the last element), the swap copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment), leaving the freed pointer in place. Probability = 1/cookie_num per eat call.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie read length is msg_size[index]-1 (sub eax,0x1 then movsxd). If msg_size[index] is 0, subtraction yields -1, movsxd sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as read count — but read() syscall treats size_t, so this would be a very large read.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether the signed JL comparison on a %llu-scanned value actually allows negative indexing at runtime — depends on compiler codegen semantics of comparing unsigned-scanned value with signed JL\",\"reason\":\"The scanf stores full 64-bit unsigned value; JL compares as signed. Need S2/S3 dynamic verification to confirm a large unsigned value passes the check and produces OOB access.\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's swap-remove leaves a exploitable dangling pointer (UAF) depends on whether create_cookie reuses the slot and whether the freed pointer is accessible via edit/read before overwrite\",\"reason\":\"Requires S2 control flow analysis of create-after-eat sequences and whether the dangling slot index is reachable.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"seccomp/sandbox status unknown — no seccomp filter detected in static analysis but not runtime-verified\",\"reason\":\"No ptrace/seccomp syscalls observed in binary; would need runtime check.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Exact heap layout and whether tcache/fastbin placement can be controlled for exploitation\",\"reason\":\"Heap allocation sizes are user-controlled (1-256 via create_cookie). Glibc 2.39 uses tcache. Specific heap feng shui requires S3 dynamic analysis.\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size OOB read (when index is negative/OOB) yields a useful value for controlling read length in edit_cookie\",\"reason\":\"Depends on what data lies at msg_size[out_of_bounds_index], which requires knowing runtime .bss content.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped. Entry=0x1110. 11 program headers, 29 sections. BuildID=84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2. GCC 10.2.0. Source: fortunecookie.c.\",\"sections\":\".text@0x1110(0x891,RX) .rodata@0x2000(0x266,R) .got@0x3f58(0xa8,RW,FullRELRO) .data@0x4000(0x10,RW) .bss@0x4020(0x1d0,RW) .plt@0x1020(0xe0,RX) .init@0x1000 .fini@0x19a4 .init_array@0x3d58 .fini_array@0x3d60 .dynamic@0x3d68 .eh_frame@0x22e0 .eh_frame_hdr@0x2268\",\"symbols\":\"main@0x187f init@0x1232 create_cookie@0x1399 eat_cookie@0x14e1 edit_cookie@0x1628 read_cookie@0x1751 menu@0x17fd _abort@0x11f5. Globals: msg@0x4060(256B) msg_size@0x4160(128B) urandom@0x41e0(8B) cookie_num@0x41e8(8B) stdout@0x4020 stdin@0x4030 stderr@0x4040\",\"disassembly\":\"main: loop menu+scanf(%lld)+dispatch(1=eat,2=create,3=edit,4=read,5=exit). init: setvbuf*3+alarm(60)+fopen(/dev/urandom)+strdup*5->msg[0..4]+cookie_num=5. create_cookie: check cookie_num<=31, scanf(%lld) len<=0x100, calloc(len,1), read(0,buf,len), strip \\\\n, msg[cookie_num]=ptr, msg_size[cookie_num]=len, cookie_num++. eat_cookie: fread 1B from urandom, idx=byte%cookie_num, printf(msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--. edit_cookie: printf Which cookie, scanf(%llu) idx, cmp idx,cookie_num; jl ok (NO lower bound), read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1), strip \\\\n. read_cookie: printf Which cookie, scanf(%llu) idx, cmp idx,cookie_num; jl ok (NO lower bound), printf(%s,msg[idx]).\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_INDEX_CHECK\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie and read_cookie, the cookie index is read via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as an unsigned 64-bit integer, but the bounds check 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' uses the signed JL (jump if less) instruction. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is interpreted as -1 in signed comparison, which is less than cookie_num (always positive), so the upper-bound check is bypassed. There is no lower-bound check at all. The attacker-controlled index is then used to access the msg[] pointer array and msg_size[] int array out of bounds, leading to OOB pointer dereference and OOB size read.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (char* array at 0x4060, 32 elements, 256 bytes in .bss)\",\"msg_size (int32 array at 0x4160, 32 elements, 128 bytes in .bss)\",\"cookie_num (int64 at 0x41e8 in .bss)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") — unsigned 64-bit integer from stdin\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker sends menu choice 3 (edit) or 4 (read)\",\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &index) stores full 64-bit unsigned value on stack at [rbp-0x10]\",\"index loaded into rdx: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]'\",\"cookie_num loaded into rax: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]'\",\"Comparison: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl skip_abort' — JL is signed less-than; if index is interpreted as negative signed, it passes\",\"In edit_cookie: index used to compute msg_size[index] via 'lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax]' — OOB read of size value\",\"In edit_cookie: index used to compute msg[index] via 'lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]' — OOB pointer dereference\",\"In edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) — OOB write to whatever address the OOB pointer contains\",\"In read_cookie: index used to compute msg[index] via same addressing — OOB pointer dereference\",\"In read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) — OOB read printed to stdout\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as 0 <= index < cookie_num, using unsigned comparison (JBE/JB) or explicit lower+upper bound check\",\"actual\":\"Only upper bound checked via 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed JL). No lower bound check. scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") stores unsigned 64-bit value, but JL treats it as signed.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). Incorrect comparison type: signed JL instead of unsigned JB/JBE on a value read as unsigned. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (-1 signed) passes 'jl' because -1 < cookie_num.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access via signedness mismatch\",\"address\":\"edit_cookie: 0x1681 (cmp/jl check), 0x16a7-0x16de (msg_size[index] and msg[index] dereference + read write); read_cookie: 0x17aa (cmp/jl check), 0x17bb-0x17e1 (msg[index] dereference + printf)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) where index is OOB; read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) where index is OOB\",\"target\":\"msg[] array at 0x4060 (32 x 8-byte pointers) and msg_size[] array at 0x4160 (32 x 4-byte ints) in .bss. Negative indices reach .bss data before msg (0x4058 and earlier), potentially reaching .data (0x4000) and .got (0x3f58) regions.\",\"size\":\"edit_cookie: write size = msg_size[index]-1 (OOB int value, potentially very large if OOB slot contains 0 — see BUG-003); read_cookie: read size unbounded (printf %s reads until null terminator)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, a negative/OOB index causes msg[index] to be read from out-of-bounds .bss memory. The value at that location is used as the destination pointer for read(0, ptr, msg_size[index]-1). If the OOB pointer value is a valid writable address, attacker-controlled data from stdin is written there. The write size comes from msg_size[index] which is also OOB.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, a negative/OOB index causes msg[index] to be read from out-of-bounds .bss memory. The value at that location is passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\", ptr), which dereferences it and prints memory content until a null byte. This leaks memory content to stdout.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) in read_cookie with an OOB index can print the content of an arbitrary pointer found in .bss, potentially leaking heap addresses, libc addresses, or other sensitive data stored in .bss/.data sections.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB pointer value at msg[index] is not a valid mapped address, dereferencing it (in read() or printf()) causes SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1671\",\"description\":\"scanf format string at rodata 0x21c4 is \\\"%llu\\\" (unsigned long long), confirmed by objdump -s .rodata showing bytes 25 6c 6c 75 at offset 0x21c4. The scanned value is stored as full 64-bit unsigned.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]' (user index), 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]' (cookie_num), 'cmp rdx, rax', 'jl 0x1692'. JL (0F 8C) is jump-if-less signed. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is -1 signed, which is < cookie_num (positive), so the check passes. No lower-bound check exists.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16de\",\"description\":\"OOB index used for array access: 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0]' + 'lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] # 4160 <msg_size>' computes &msg_size[index] (OOB if index negative). 'mov eax,[rdx+rax]' reads OOB int. Then 'sub eax,0x1; movsxd rdx,eax' computes read length. 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0]' + 'lea rax,[rip+0x298e] # 4060 <msg>' computes &msg[index] (OOB). 'mov rax,[rcx+rax]' dereferences OOB pointer. 'call read@plt' writes to that pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179a-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Same bounds check pattern as edit_cookie: 'mov rdx, QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10]', 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]', 'cmp rdx, rax', 'jl 0x17bb'. Same signed JL on unsigned-scanned value. No lower-bound check.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"OOB index used: 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]' + 'lea rax,[rip+0x2892] # 4060 <msg>' computes &msg[index] (OOB). 'mov rax,[rdx+rax]' dereferences OOB pointer. 'mov rsi,rax' passes it as printf arg. Format string at 0x21a9 is \\\"%s\\\".\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"description\":\"msg array at 0x4060, msg_size at 0x4160, both in .bss. Negative index -1 accesses msg[-1] at 0x4058 (8 bytes before msg, in .bss padding). Larger negative indices reach .data (0x4000) and .got (0x3f58). The .bss layout is: stdout(0x4020), stdin(0x4030), stderr(0x4040), completed.0(0x4048), [padding 0x4049-0x405f], msg(0x4060). Index -1 → 0x4058, index -3 → 0x4048 (completed.0), index -4 → 0x4040 (stderr FILE*), index -5 → 0x4038, index -6 → 0x4030 (stdin FILE*), index -8 → 0x4020 (stdout FILE*). These FILE* pointers are valid heap addresses, so read_cookie would print FILE struct content (info leak), and edit_cookie would write to the FILE struct (memory corruption).\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Dynamic verification that scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") with input like 18446744073709551615 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) actually passes the JL check at runtime\",\"Dynamic verification of what memory content is at msg[-1] through msg[-8] at runtime (FILE* pointers in .bss)\",\"Dynamic verification that the OOB pointer dereference in read_cookie produces readable output or crash\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"HEAP_USE_AFTER_FREE_VIA_SWAP_REMOVE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, after free(msg[idx]), the function performs swap-remove: msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] and msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1], then cookie_num--. However, msg[cookie_num-1] (the old last slot) is NOT cleared/nullified after the swap. When idx == cookie_num-1 (self-swap case), the freed pointer is copied onto itself, leaving the dangling pointer in the slot. Even when idx != cookie_num-1, the old last slot retains the freed pointer value. After cookie_num is decremented, the dangling slot is at index cookie_num (now out of the valid range), but a subsequent create_cookie increments cookie_num and writes to msg[cookie_num] — if the new cookie_num equals the old freed slot index, the slot is overwritten before use. However, if the self-swap case occurs (idx == cookie_num-1), the freed pointer remains at the SAME index that is still within the valid range [0, cookie_num-1], making it directly reachable via edit_cookie or read_cookie without needing create_cookie.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (char* array at 0x4060 in .bss)\",\"cookie_num (int64 at 0x41e8 in .bss)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom (1 byte, modulo cookie_num)\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie: fread 1 byte from /dev/urandom into stack var\",\"idx = byte % cookie_num (via idiv at 0x153e)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) — prints cookie content\",\"free(msg[idx]) at 0x158e — heap buffer freed\",\"msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15b0-0x15c6 — swap last element into freed slot\",\"msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15e0-0x15fc — swap size\",\"cookie_num-- at 0x1606-0x160a\",\"If idx == old cookie_num-1 (self-swap): msg[idx] still holds the freed pointer (self-assignment)\",\"The slot idx is still in valid range [0, new cookie_num-1] since idx < old cookie_num and new cookie_num = old cookie_num - 1, so idx <= new cookie_num - 1\",\"Attacker can now call edit_cookie(idx) or read_cookie(idx) to access the freed buffer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After free(msg[idx]), the freed slot should be cleared (msg[idx] = NULL) or the swap should also null out the old last slot (msg[cookie_num-1] = NULL).\",\"actual\":\"Swap copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] but does NOT null msg[cookie_num-1]. In self-swap case (idx == cookie_num-1), the freed pointer remains in the slot.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing nullification of the source slot after swap-copy. The freed pointer remains reachable when self-swap occurs.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Dangling pointer dereference after free\",\"address\":\"eat_cookie: free at 0x158e, swap at 0x15b0-0x15c6, no null at old slot\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; /* msg[cookie_num-1] NOT cleared */\",\"target\":\"Heap buffer previously allocated by calloc in create_cookie or strdup in init\",\"size\":\"Original allocation size (1-256 bytes for create_cookie, or strdup length for init cookies)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed heap buffer is accessed via read_cookie after eat_cookie self-swap, printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) reads from the freed buffer. If the heap allocator has reused the memory for another allocation, this reads data from the new allocation (info leak). If not yet reused, reads stale data.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed heap buffer is accessed via edit_cookie after eat_cookie self-swap, read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) writes attacker-controlled data into the freed buffer. If the heap allocator has reused the memory, this corrupts the new allocation.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Writing to a freed heap buffer that has been reallocated for a different purpose corrupts the new allocation's data, potentially affecting heap metadata or other heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed buffer's memory has been unmapped or if heap metadata is corrupted by the write, subsequent heap operations may crash.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"free(msg[idx]) called: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1]' loads msg[idx], 'mov rdi,rax', 'call free@plt'. The pointer is freed.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1593-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Swap-remove: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]; sub rax,0x1' computes cookie_num-1. 'lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]' loads msg[cookie_num-1]. Then 'lea rcx,[rdx*8]; lea rdx,[rip+msg]; mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx],rax' stores it into msg[idx]. There is NO instruction that sets msg[cookie_num-1] to NULL after the copy.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1530-0x1544\",\"description\":\"idx = fread_byte % cookie_num. When cookie_num is N, idx can be 0..N-1. The probability that idx == cookie_num-1 (self-swap) is 1/N per eat call. With initial cookie_num=5, probability is 1/5 = 20% per eat call. After multiple eat calls, the cumulative probability of at least one self-swap increases.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1606-0x160a\",\"description\":\"After swap, cookie_num is decremented: 'mov rax, QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num]; sub rax,0x1; mov QWORD PTR [rip+cookie_num],rax'. In the self-swap case (idx == old_cookie_num-1), after decrement the new cookie_num = old_cookie_num - 1 = idx. So idx == new cookie_num, which means idx is now OUT of valid range [0, new_cookie_num-1]. Wait — idx = old_cookie_num-1, new_cookie_num = old_cookie_num-1, so idx = new_cookie_num, which is NOT < new_cookie_num. So the self-swap slot is actually at index == new cookie_num, which is out of range. BUT: if the attacker then calls create_cookie, cookie_num increments back to old_cookie_num, and msg[old_cookie_num-1] (the dangling slot) is overwritten by the new allocation. So the UAF window is: after eat (self-swap), before next create at that slot — the dangling pointer is at index == cookie_num, which is NOT directly reachable via edit/read (which check index < cookie_num). The UAF is reachable only if create_cookie is called (incrementing cookie_num, making the slot valid again) but create_cookie OVERWRITES the slot with a new pointer. So the dangling pointer is overwritten before it can be accessed. Revised: the self-swap case does NOT leave a directly reachable dangling pointer because the slot index equals the new cookie_num (out of range). The non-self-swap case leaves the dangling pointer at old cookie_num-1, which is also now == new cookie_num (out of range). In both cases, the dangling slot is at index == cookie_num after decrement, which is out of the valid range. The dangling pointer becomes reachable only if create_cookie is called, but create_cookie writes to msg[cookie_num] (overwriting the dangling pointer) before incrementing. So the dangling pointer is always overwritten before becoming reachable. This means the UAF is NOT directly exploitable through normal edit/read paths.\"}],\"confidence\":\"LOW\",\"verification_required\":[\"Dynamic verification of whether the dangling pointer at msg[old_cookie_num-1] can be reached before create_cookie overwrites it — specifically whether any code path accesses msg[cookie_num] without first writing to it\",\"Analysis of whether the OOB signedness bug (BUG-001) can be combined with eat_cookie to reach the dangling slot at index == cookie_num (which would pass the signed JL check if interpreted as a large unsigned value, but cookie_num is small and positive, so index == cookie_num would NOT pass JL since cookie_num is not < cookie_num)\",\"Re-examination of whether there is any other code path that accesses msg[] at index == cookie_num without going through create_cookie first\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_READ_LENGTH\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, the read length is computed as msg_size[index] - 1. The instruction sequence is: 'mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax]' (load msg_size[index] as int32), 'sub eax, 0x1' (subtract 1), 'movsxd rdx, eax' (sign-extend int32 to int64). If msg_size[index] is 0, the subtraction yields -1 (0xFFFFFFFF as int32), and movsxd sign-extends it to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (int64). This value is passed as the size_t count argument to read(0, buf, count). The read() syscall interprets size_t as unsigned, so 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is a massive read count, allowing a buffer overflow far beyond the allocated buffer size.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size (int32 array at 0x4160 in .bss)\",\"msg (char* array at 0x4060 in .bss)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") — combined with BUG-001 OOB access, or with a valid index whose msg_size happens to be 0\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker calls edit_cookie with an index (possibly OOB via BUG-001)\",\"msg_size[index] is loaded as int32: 'mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax]' at 0x16b6\",\"Subtract 1: 'sub eax, 0x1' at 0x16b9 — if msg_size[index] == 0, eax becomes -1 (0xFFFFFFFF)\",\"Sign-extend to 64-bit: 'movsxd rdx, eax' at 0x16bc — rdx becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"This is passed as the count argument to read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de\",\"read() interprets count as size_t (unsigned), attempting to read up to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF bytes into msg[index] buffer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The read length should be validated to be > 0 before use, or msg_size[index] should be checked to be >= 1 before subtracting 1.\",\"actual\":\"No validation of msg_size[index] before subtraction. If msg_size[index] is 0 (which can happen via OOB access to uninitialized .bss memory, or if a cookie with length 0 is created), the subtraction underflows.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing check for msg_size[index] > 0 before computing msg_size[index] - 1. No clamping of the read length to the actual buffer size.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Integer underflow leading to oversized read\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], (size_t)(int64)(msg_size[index] - 1)) where msg_size[index] can be 0\",\"target\":\"Heap buffer pointed to by msg[index]\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (if msg_size[index] == 0), effectively unbounded\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If msg_size[index] is 0 (via OOB access to .bss memory containing zeros, or via a valid cookie with length 0), the read length underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. The read() call writes attacker-controlled data from stdin into the heap buffer at msg[index] with effectively no size limit, overflowing into adjacent heap memory.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The oversized write corrupts heap memory beyond the allocated buffer, potentially damaging heap metadata and adjacent heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Writing beyond the heap buffer will eventually hit unmapped memory, causing SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16bc\",\"description\":\"Read length computation: 'mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax]' loads msg_size[index] as 32-bit int. 'sub eax, 0x1' subtracts 1. If msg_size[index] == 0, eax = 0xFFFFFFFF (-1 as int32). 'movsxd rdx, eax' sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. This is passed as the 3rd argument (rdx) to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"read(0, msg[index], rdx) where rdx = sign-extended (msg_size[index] - 1). The read@plt call at 0x1090 receives this as size_t count. No bounds check on the computed length before the call.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4160\",\"description\":\"msg_size array at 0x4160 in .bss. For OOB indices (via BUG-001), msg_size[negative_index] accesses .bss memory before msg_size. The region 0x4000-0x415f includes .data (0x4000-0x400f) and .bss padding (0x4049-0x405f) and msg array content (0x4060-0x415f). Many of these bytes are zero (uninitialized .bss), so msg_size[OOB_index] is likely 0, triggering the underflow. Even for in-bounds indices, if create_cookie is called with length=0 (scanf %lld reads 0, which passes the <=0x100 check), calloc(0,1) returns a valid pointer, msg_size[slot]=0, and edit_cookie on that slot triggers the underflow.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Dynamic verification that create_cookie accepts length=0 (scanf %lld reads 0, cmp 0,0x100; jle passes, calloc(0,1) returns non-NULL)\",\"Dynamic verification that edit_cookie on a slot with msg_size=0 produces the underflow and oversized read\",\"Dynamic verification of what .bss memory values exist at OOB msg_size indices at runtime\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"Length check and buffer allocation\",\"reason\":\"create_cookie checks length <= 0x100 via 'cmp rax,0x100; jle' (signed JLE, but 0x100 is small positive, so signed/unsigned doesn't matter for values 0-256). calloc(length,1) zeros memory. read(0,buf,length) uses the same length. The newline strip writes buf[read_ret]=0 which is within bounds (read_ret <= length). No overflow in the allocation or read. Note: length=0 is valid (passes <=0x100), calloc(0,1) returns a minimum-size allocation, but the read(0,buf,0) reads 0 bytes — no overflow here. The issue with length=0 arises in edit_cookie (BUG-003), not in create_cookie itself.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu dispatch\",\"reason\":\"main reads choice via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and dispatches 1-5 using cmp/jne. Choices outside 1-5 fall through to the loop again (no action). No vulnerability in the dispatch logic.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Initialization\",\"reason\":\"init calls setvbuf, alarm(60), fopen, and strdup 5 fortune strings. All sizes are hardcoded constants. No user input. No vulnerability.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie swap-remove UAF (BUG-002) is directly exploitable\",\"reason\":\"Static analysis shows that after eat_cookie, the dangling pointer is at index == new cookie_num (out of valid range for edit/read). create_cookie overwrites this slot before it becomes reachable. The self-swap case also leaves the pointer at index == new cookie_num. No code path was found that accesses msg[cookie_num] without first writing to it via create_cookie. However, the OOB signedness bug (BUG-001) might allow reaching index == cookie_num if a large unsigned value is interpreted as a small positive number — but this is not possible since the value must be < cookie_num (signed JL), and cookie_num itself is a small positive number, so index must be strictly less than cookie_num.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic verification: after eat_cookie self-swap, attempt to access the dangling slot via edit_cookie or read_cookie with index == cookie_num to confirm it is rejected by the bounds check. Also verify whether any sequence of eat/create operations can leave a dangling pointer at an in-range index.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB access via BUG-001 can reach useful data in .got or .data sections\",\"reason\":\"msg is at 0x4060. .got is at 0x3f58 (0x108 bytes before msg, i.e., index -33 to -34 approximately). .data is at 0x4000 (0x60 bytes before msg, index -12 to -13). The exact values at these OOB indices at runtime (FILE* pointers, GOT entries) need dynamic verification to determine what pointer values are dereferenced and what sizes are read.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic verification: run the binary and dump .bss/.data/.got content at OOB msg[] indices to determine what pointer values and msg_size values exist at negative indices.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":4,\"verified\":4,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in index bounds check: scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") reads unsigned 64-bit index, but comparison uses signed JL. Large unsigned values (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1 signed) pass the check, causing OOB access to msg[] and msg_size[] arrays.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie uses printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) where index passes the signed JL bounds check but is actually a large unsigned value interpreted as negative, accessing msg[] out of bounds. The OOB pointer (e.g. stdout FILE* at msg[-8]) is dereferenced and its content is printed to stdout.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie/read_cookie index check\",\"scanf(%llu) stores large unsigned value (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1 signed)\",\"Signed JL comparison passes (-1 < cookie_num)\",\"msg[index] dereferences OOB .bss memory (e.g. msg[-8] = stdout FILE*)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) prints FILE struct content to stdout\",\"INFO_LEAK: libc FILE structure data leaked\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Read target address (via OOB index selection)\",\"how\":\"Attacker selects which negative index to pass to read_cookie. Each negative index maps to a specific .bss global variable (stdout at -8, stdin at -6, stderr at -4, __dso_handle at -11). The value at that .bss slot is a FILE* pointer into libc, and printf(\\\"%s\\\", ptr) dereferences it and prints content until null byte.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"OOB index must pass signed JL bounds check: index (as signed int64) < cookie_num. Any value 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (=-1) through 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFB (=-5) works when cookie_num=5.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the only bounds check\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[index] must contain a valid readable pointer. Indices -8 (stdout), -6 (stdin), -4 (stderr) contain FILE* pointers into libc which are always mapped and readable.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf to dereference without crashing\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The leaked data must contain non-null bytes before the first null byte. FILE struct _flags field (0xfbad2887) has 4 non-null bytes before the first 0x00, producing a 4-byte leak.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to observe leaked data in stdout\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(msg_base + (-8)*8) = *(0x4060 - 64) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*\",\"alias\":\"stdout@@GLIBC_2.2.5 global variable at .bss 0x4020\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(-8) dereferences stdout FILE* and prints _IO_2_1_stdout_._flags = 0xfbad2887, leaking libc internal data\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-6] = *(0x4030) = stdin FILE*, msg[-4] = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE*\",\"alias\":\"stdin/stderr FILE* pointers in .bss\",\"implication\":\"Different OOB indices leak different FILE struct data, proving address-controlled read\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle (self-referencing: value == own address)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle at .data 0x4008\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(-11) leaks __dso_handle address, revealing PIE base (addr - 0x4008 = PIE base)\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with OOB index -8 (stdout FILE*) and observe leaked FILE struct _flags bytes in stdout output. Repeat with indices -6 (stdin) and -4 (stderr) to confirm address-controlled leak.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"stdout FILE struct _flags=0xfbad2887 leaked (4 bytes: 87 28 ad fb)\",\"stdin FILE struct _flags=0xfbad208b leaked (4 bytes: 8b 20 ad fb)\",\"stderr FILE struct _flags=0xfbad2087 leaked (4 bytes: 87 20 ad fb)\",\"Different indices produce different leaked data = address-controlled\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base address, but the OOB leak of __dso_handle (index -11) reveals the PIE base, defeating ASLR for the binary itself.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only after relocation, but does not prevent reading GOT entries via OOB index (info leak of libc addresses).\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution from stack/heap, but does not affect info leak primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary protects return addresses, irrelevant to this .bss OOB read primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-8) output: bytes 87 28 ad fb = _IO_2_1_stdout_._flags = 0xfbad2887. Confirmed FILE struct content leaked via OOB pointer dereference.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-6) output: bytes 8b 20 ad fb = _IO_2_1_stdin_._flags = 0xfbad208b. Different index leaks different data, confirming address-controlled read.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-11) output: 6 bytes of __dso_handle address (e.g. 08 70 89 dc cb 5f = 0x5fcbdc897008). Leaks PIE base address.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2887 (stdout _flags), 0xfbad208b (stdin _flags), 0xfbad2087 (stderr _flags), PIE base (via __dso_handle)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-001 VERIFIED: read_cookie OOB index -8 leaks stdout FILE struct _flags=0xfbad2887. PRIM-001b VERIFIED: indices -8/-6/-4 leak different FILE struct data (0xfbad2887/0xfbad208b/0xfbad2087), confirming address-controlled info leak. Additionally, index -11 leaks __dso_handle address revealing PIE base.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT: the OOB index directly selects which .bss pointer to dereference, and printf(\\\"%s\\\") prints the pointed-to memory. No intermediate object reuse or heap manipulation needed. The leak reveals libc FILE structure internals and PIE base address.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check allows OOB negative index. msg[-11] = __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer at .data 0x4008). msg_size[-11] aliases the high 4 bytes of msg[26] (a heap pointer), which is 0x00005555 = 21845 (small positive). edit_cookie(-11) calls read(0, __dso_handle, 21844), writing attacker data to __dso_handle.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie uses read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) where index is OOB. For index -11: msg[-11] = __dso_handle (self-referencing, writable), msg_size[-11] = 0x00005555 (high half of msg[26] heap pointer after creating 24 cookies). The write target is __dso_handle at a fixed .bss/.data address.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check\",\"Create 24 cookies to fill msg[5..28], msg[26] gets heap pointer 0x0000555555xxxxxx\",\"msg_size[-11] aliases high 4 bytes of msg[26] = 0x00005555 = 21845 (small positive)\",\"msg[-11] = __dso_handle = 0x555555558008 (self-referencing, writable)\",\"edit_cookie(-11): read(0, 0x555555558008, 21844) writes attacker data to __dso_handle\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE: attacker-controlled data written to fixed .data address (__dso_handle)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write content (attacker data from stdin) and write target (fixed: __dso_handle at PIE+0x4008)\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends data via stdin which read() writes to __dso_handle. The write target is fixed (__dso_handle address), not attacker-controlled. The write size (21844) is determined by msg_size[-11] aliasing.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"OOB index -11 must pass signed JL check: -11 < cookie_num (5). 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5 as signed = -11 < 5, passes.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass bounds check\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-11] must be a small positive value (not 0, not huge). This requires msg[26] to contain a heap pointer whose high 4 bytes = 0x00005555. Achieved by creating 24 cookies (indices 5-28), filling msg[26] with a heap pointer.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to avoid integer underflow (msg_size=0 -> count=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF -> EFAULT) and to get a reasonable write size\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] must be a valid writable address. __dso_handle at .data 0x4008 is in the writable .data section and is self-referencing (value == own address).\",\"necessity\":\"Required for read() to successfully write\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"After read() returns, the newline-strip code re-reads msg[-11] (now overwritten) and dereferences *(overwritten_value + read_ret). The overwritten value must be a valid readable address to avoid crash. Writing a valid .bss address (e.g. msg array address) satisfies this.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for program to survive Step 1 and continue to Step 2\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(msg_size_base + (-11)*4) = *(0x4160 - 44) = *(0x4134)\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[26] (at 0x4060 + 26*8 = 0x4130, bytes 4-7 at 0x4134)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-11] aliases the high half of msg[26]'s 8-byte heap pointer. Heap pointers are 0x0000555555xxxxxx, so high 4 bytes = 0x00005555 = 21845. This is a small positive value that passes as a valid read count (21844 after -1).\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(msg_base + (-11)*8) = *(0x4060 - 88) = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable at .data 0x4008\",\"implication\":\"__dso_handle is self-referencing (its value equals its own address 0x555555558008). Writing to it modifies the pointer that msg[-11] reads, enabling the two-step ARB_WRITE (PRIM-003).\"},{\"expression\":\"Creating 24 cookies (indices 5-28) fills msg[26] with heap pointer\",\"alias\":\"msg[26] = calloc heap pointer = 0x0000555555xxxxxx\",\"implication\":\"The setup precondition: 24 create_cookie calls are needed before msg_size[-11] becomes non-zero (0x00005555). Before this setup, msg_size[-11] = 0, causing underflow to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and EFAULT.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 24 cookies to fill msg[26] with heap pointer (sets msg_size[-11]=0x5555). 2. Leak PIE base via read_cookie(-11) (__dso_handle self-reference). 3. edit_cookie(-11) writes msg_addr (valid .bss address) to __dso_handle. 4. read_cookie(-11) confirms read target changed (different bytes leaked).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"PIE base leaked via __dso_handle self-reference\",\"__dso_handle overwritten: leak changes from 08xx (addr ending 4008) to 80xx (heap pointer at msg[0])\",\"Write size = 21844 (0x5554 = 0x5555-1)\",\"Program survives (no crash) after writing valid readable address\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes __dso_handle address, but the self-referencing property means read_cookie(-11) leaks the address, defeating PIE.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO does not protect .data section variables like __dso_handle. The write target is in .data, not .got, so RELRO is irrelevant.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX does not prevent data writes to .data/.bss.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary is irrelevant to .bss/.data OOB write.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed at read() call: rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle), rdx=0x5554 (21844). read(0, __dso_handle, 21844) writes to __dso_handle.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"After edit_cookie(-11) with p64(msg_addr): read_cookie(-11) leak changed from 087089dccb5f (original __dso_handle=0x...4008) to 80645703cc5f (msg[0] heap pointer=0x...a480). Write confirmed.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16bc\",\"description\":\"msg_size[index] computed via lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax]; sub eax,1; movsxd rdx,eax. For index=-11: reads 4 bytes at msg_size[-11] = 0x00005555, subtracts 1 = 0x5554 = 21844.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x00005fcbdc897060 (msg_addr, a valid .bss address)\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-002 VERIFIED: edit_cookie(-11) with 24 cookies created writes to __dso_handle. GDB confirmed read(0, 0x555555558008, 21844). After writing msg_addr, read_cookie(-11) leak changed from 087089dccb5f to 80645703cc5f, confirming __dso_handle was overwritten. Write target is fixed (__dso_handle at PIE+0x4008), write content is attacker-controlled.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE is DIRECT: the OOB index directly selects __dso_handle as the write target. The write target is fixed (not attacker-controlled), so this is RELATIVE_WRITE, not ARB_WRITE. The key alias finding: msg_size[-11] aliases the high 4 bytes of msg[26]'s heap pointer (0x00005555 = 21845), which is a small positive value that avoids the integer underflow that kills the write when msg_size[-11]=0. Setup requires 24 create_cookie calls.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check allows OOB negative index -11, which selects __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer). Two-step write: Step 1 overwrites __dso_handle with target address Y; Step 2 uses same edit_cookie(-11) which now writes to Y.\",\"description\":\"__dso_handle at .data 0x4008 is self-referencing (value == own address). edit_cookie(-11) writes to *(__dso_handle). Step 1: write target address Y to __dso_handle (overwrites the pointer). Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again, now msg[-11] = Y, so read(0, Y, 21844) writes attacker data to arbitrary address Y.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check\",\"Create 24 cookies -> msg_size[-11] = 0x00005555 (high half of msg[26] heap pointer)\",\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes target address Y to __dso_handle (self-referencing slot)\",\"__dso_handle now contains Y instead of its own address\",\"Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again: msg[-11] = __dso_handle = Y\",\"read(0, Y, 21844) writes attacker data to attacker-specified address Y\",\"ARB_WRITE: Write-What-Where (attacker controls both target Y and written data)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write target address Y (attacker-specified) AND write content (attacker data from stdin)\",\"how\":\"Step 1: attacker sends p64(Y) as data to edit_cookie(-11), which writes Y to __dso_handle. Step 2: attacker sends payload data to edit_cookie(-11), which now writes to *Y. Two different Y values (Y1=msg[0] addr, Y2=msg[1] addr) both verified.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"All preconditions from PRIM-002 (RELATIVE_WRITE) must be satisfied: 24 cookies created, msg_size[-11]=0x5555, msg[-11]=__dso_handle.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for the base write primitive to work\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"Step 1 write target (Y) must be a valid readable address. After Step 1, __dso_handle = Y. The newline-strip code re-reads msg[-11] = Y and dereferences *(Y + read_ret). Y must be readable at offset read_ret (typically 8). Writing a .bss address (e.g. msg array) satisfies this.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for program to survive Step 1 and proceed to Step 2\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"Step 2 write target (Y) must be a valid writable address. read(0, Y, 21844) writes to Y. .bss addresses (msg array) are writable.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for Step 2 write to succeed\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"After Step 2, the newline-strip code re-reads msg[-11] = Y (still the same Y from Step 1) and dereferences *(Y + read_ret). Y must be readable at the new offset. Since Y is a .bss address, this is satisfied.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for program to survive Step 2\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"PIE base must be known to compute Y. Leaked via read_cookie(-11) which reads __dso_handle (self-referencing, reveals PIE+0x4008).\",\"necessity\":\"Required to compute target address Y in PIE binary\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"__dso_handle value == &__dso_handle (self-referencing pointer)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle at .data 0x4008, value = 0x555555558008 = &__dso_handle\",\"implication\":\"Writing to *(__dso_handle) modifies __dso_handle itself. This is the key property enabling two-step ARB_WRITE: Step 1 changes the pointer, Step 2 writes through the changed pointer.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of msg[26] = 0x00005555 = 21845\",\"alias\":\"Heap pointer high half aliases msg_size[-11]\",\"implication\":\"The write count (21844) is large enough to write 8 bytes (a full pointer) in both steps, but small enough to pass kernel access_ok check (unlike 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF which causes EFAULT).\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 24 cookies. 2. Leak PIE base via read_cookie(-11). 3. Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes Y1 (msg[0] address) to __dso_handle. 4. Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) writes 0x4141414141414141 to Y1. 5. read_cookie(0) crashes (SIGSEGV at 0x4141...). 6. Repeat with Y2 (msg[1] address) and value 0x4141414141414142. Both targets verified.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Y1=0x...d060: wrote 0x4141414141414141, read_cookie(0) crashed (SIGSEGV, signal 11)\",\"Y2=0x...7068: wrote 0x4141414141414142, read_cookie(1) crashed (SIGSEGV, signal 11)\",\"Two different Y values both written = address-controlled write\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses, but the self-referencing __dso_handle leak reveals PIE base, allowing computation of any binary address as Y.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but the ARB_WRITE target Y can be any writable address (.bss, heap, stack), not limited to GOT.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents executing written shellcode, but does not prevent data writes.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary is irrelevant to .bss/heap writes.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"Step 1 for Y1: edit_cookie(-11) wrote msg_addr (0x000055a38fecd060) to __dso_handle. Program printed 'Done!' and survived (newline-strip read *(msg_addr+8) = msg[1], valid).\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"Step 2 for Y1: edit_cookie(-11) wrote 0x4141414141414141 to Y1=0x000055a38fecd060 (msg[0]). Program printed 'Done!' and survived. read_cookie(0) then crashed with SIGSEGV (signal 11) because msg[0]=0x4141414141414141 is not a valid address for printf dereference.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"Step 1+2 for Y2: Same process with Y2=0x000058342cea7068 (msg[1]). Wrote 0x4141414141414142 to Y2. read_cookie(1) crashed with SIGSEGV (signal 11). Two different Y values both written = ARB_WRITE confirmed.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"Y1: 0x4141414141414141 at 0x000055a38fecd060; Y2: 0x4141414141414142 at 0x000058342cea7068\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"Y1: ~0x4141414141414141; Y2: ~0x4141414141414142\",\"return_code\":-11,\"details\":\"PRIM-003 VERIFIED: Two-step ARB_WRITE via self-referencing __dso_handle. Y1=0x...d060 (msg[0]): wrote 0x4141414141414141, read_cookie(0) crashed SIGSEGV. Y2=0x...7068 (msg[1]): wrote 0x4141414141414142, read_cookie(1) crashed SIGSEGV. Two different attacker-specified addresses written with two different attacker-specified values. Both crashes at the written values confirm Write-What-Where.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"ARB_WRITE is INDIRECT: requires two steps through the self-referencing __dso_handle pointer. Step 1 modifies the pointer (write to slot P), Step 2 writes through the modified pointer (write to *P == Y). The self-referencing property of __dso_handle (value == &value) is the key enabler: writing to *(__dso_handle) modifies __dso_handle itself, so the same OOB index (-11) serves both steps. The write count (21844) is sufficient and passes kernel access_ok, unlike the msg_size=0 underflow case (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF -> EFAULT).\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-004\",\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"OOB signedness mismatch allows negative index. msg[-1] = 0x0000000000000000 (NULL, .bss padding). read_cookie(-1) calls printf(\\\"%s\\\", NULL) which is a NULL pointer dereference.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with OOB index -1 accesses msg[-1] at .bss 0x4058, which contains 0x0000000000000000 (uninitialized .bss padding). printf(\\\"%s\\\", NULL) either crashes (SIGSEGV) or prints '(null)' depending on glibc version.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in read_cookie index check\",\"Index -1 passes signed JL check (-1 < cookie_num)\",\"msg[-1] = *(0x4058) = 0x0000000000000000 (NULL, .bss padding)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", NULL) -> NULL pointer dereference\",\"CRASH / NULL_POINTER_DEREF\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Crash trigger (via OOB index selection)\",\"how\":\"Attacker selects index -1 which maps to a NULL pointer in .bss padding. The NULL is then dereferenced by printf.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":false},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"OOB index -1 must pass signed JL check: -1 < cookie_num. Always true.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass bounds check\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-1] must be NULL (0x0000000000000000). This is .bss padding between completed.0 (0x4048) and msg (0x4060), which is zero-initialized.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for NULL pointer dereference\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-1] = *(0x4060 - 8) = *(0x4058) = 0 (bss padding)\",\"alias\":\"Uninitialized .bss padding between completed.0 and msg\",\"implication\":\"NULL pointer in .bss padding, dereferenced by printf(\\\"%s\\\")\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with OOB index -1 (NULL pointer in .bss padding). Observe crash or '(null)' output.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"printf printed '(null)' (glibc handles NULL gracefully in this version)\",\"NULL pointer dereference confirmed\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE does not prevent NULL dereference from OOB index.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-1): printf(\\\"%s\\\", NULL) printed '(null)' on glibc 2.39. NULL pointer dereference confirmed (glibc handles gracefully, no SIGSEGV). Process continued normally.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"0x0 (NULL)\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-004 VERIFIED: read_cookie(-1) dereferences msg[-1]=NULL. printf(\\\"%s\\\", NULL) printed '(null)' (glibc 2.39 handles NULL gracefully). NULL pointer dereference confirmed. No crash on this glibc version, but the NULL deref is confirmed.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"On glibc 2.39, printf(\\\"%s\\\", NULL) prints '(null)' instead of crashing. The NULL pointer dereference still occurs (the pointer is read and found to be NULL), but glibc's printf implementation handles NULL gracefully. This is a NULL_POINTER_DEREF primitive, not a DOS/CRASH in this environment.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[],\"unresolved\":[]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
